WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
Sprinkle fine sand on a metal plate,

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:09.200
and draw a violin bow along its edge.

3
00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:16.350
The sand gathers on one line: the vibration splits the plate in two.

4
00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:21.766
Five friends on a bench, tied by rubber bands.

5
00:00:21.766 --> 00:00:26.566
Total pull: thickness times distance squared, added up.

6
00:00:26.566 --> 00:00:31.166
No huddling on one spot: spread out, with the least pull.

7
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.200
Let go, and the bands pull everyone into place.

8
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:44.400
Close friends are pulled together.

9
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.978
Eva, Dev left; Ben, Ada right; Cai between.

10
00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.180
Read off each person's position.

11
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:58.313
That list of numbers is the Fiedler vector.

12
00:00:58.313 --> 00:01:01.713
Close friends get close numbers.

13
00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.506
Now try a cut at every gap along the bench.

14
00:01:07.506 --> 00:01:11.417
Friendship split: 6, 3, 5, 6.

15
00:01:11.417 --> 00:01:15.994
Two or three a side: take 3, not the middle.

16
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:23.560
Multiply every number by minus one —

17
00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:26.560
the whole line mirrors over.

18
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.133
Still the same cut, with left and right renamed.

19
00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:36.133
A sign flip is not a new answer.

20
00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:46.411
What if they are two groups who never met?

21
00:01:46.411 --> 00:01:50.211
Everyone in a group lands on the same number.

22
00:01:50.211 --> 00:01:56.211
Then λ₂ = 0: the numbers only say who connects. Check connectivity first.

23
00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.959
How many ways to split five people in two?

24
00:02:02.959 --> 00:02:07.759
Left or right each: 32, minus all-left and all-right.

25
00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:12.337
Swapping sides is the same: 15, not 16.

26
00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.560
On a chip, this cut is often where partitioning starts.

27
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:27.500
One number each, from the whole graph.

28
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:33.500
Along the line, try every gap.

29
00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:40.500
What you get is a good candidate, not a guarantee.

30
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:48.500
After the cut, what about swapping people across? See the next module.

31
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:56.600
In the lab below, change a weight and watch the line move.
